		youtube: "https://youtu.be/vnF-3lhbZPc",
		blog: "Curing AI Amnesia: How I Built \"The Codebase Archaeologist\" Have you ever looked at a bizarre line of legacy code and wondered, “Who wrote this, and what on earth were they thinking?”  Usually, finding the answer involves a grueling search through Slack histories, Jira archives, and old pull request comments. Standard AI coding assistants are great at reading the code itself, but they suffer from AI Amnesia—they lack the context of the conversations and decisions that led to that code.  For \"The Hangover Part AI: Where's My Context?\" Hackathon, I set out to solve this exact problem as a solo developer by building The Codebase Archaeologist.  The Tech Stack: Giving AI a Permanent Memory The Codebase Archaeologist bridges the gap between raw source code and developer history using a hybrid graph-vector memory.  The Brain (Backend): Built with Python, FastAPI, and Cognee. Cognee is the secret sauce here—it allows us to ingest unstructured data (like Slack debates and Jira tickets) and cognify it into a permanent, structured graph database. The Face (Frontend): A React + TailwindCSS dashboard that renders an interactive, real-time node visualizer of the memory state so judges and developers can see exactly how the AI connects code back to its history. How Cognee Gives AI a True Memory Standard RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation) systems search for documents using simple semantic similarity, which often misses the structural relationships of software development.  Cognee solves this by mapping files, issues, and discussions into a semantic knowledge graph. When you ingest data, Cognee extracts key concepts, links them (e.g., SlackMessage --discussed--> PullRequest --modified--> SourceCode), and persists these links in a database. When the AI is asked about a specific function, it doesn't just read the file—it walks the graph to remember the Slack debate that created it!  My Journey as a Solo Developer Competing as a solo developer in a hackathon is always a race against the clock. Building a FastAPI backend, setting up Cognee's graph pipeline, and designing a polished React frontend visualizer from scratch required wear-many-hats velocity.  To keep up the pace, I had a bit of help along the way! I used the Antigravity AI agent (powered by Google's Gemini models) as my pair programmer. Having Gemini and Antigravity assist with setting up git, building configuration files, and streamlining components was like having a virtual teammate, letting me focus on the core architecture.",
